{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_016VDeSPmLgJa7D2vgqsu69V","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Translating the `match()` function of `pcre2_match.c`\n2\t\n3\tRead `TRANSLATION_GUIDE.md` first, then this file, then `src/matcher_core.rs`.\n4\t\n5\tThe C function `match()` (C lines 684–6950 of `c_src/src/pcre2_match.c`) is one\n6\tenormous function built around `goto`. It has been reorganised in\n7\t`src/matcher_core.rs` into a single flat dispatch loop:\n8\t\n9\t```rust\n10\tlet mut lbl: u32 = LBL_NEW_FRAME;\n11\t'sw: loop {\n12\t    if lbl == LBL_MATCH_RECURSE { /* C lines 753-846 */  lbl = LBL_NEW_FRAME; }\n13\t    if lbl == LBL_NEW_FRAME     { /* C lines 849-877 */  lbl = LBL_TOP_OF_LOOP; }\n14\t    if lbl == LBL_TOP_OF_LOOP   { (*F).op = *(*F).ecode; lbl = LBL_SWITCH; }\n15\t    if lbl == LBL_RETURN_SWITCH { /* C lines 6909-6944 */ lbl = LBL_RM_BASE + return_id; }\n16\t\n17\t    include!(\"matcher_arms/a.rs\");   // C  900-1659\n18\t    include!(\"matcher_arms/b.rs\");   // C 1660-2050\n19\t    include!(\"matcher_arms/c.rs\");   // C 2051-2573\n20\t    include!(\"matcher_arms/d.rs\");   // C 2574-2918\n21\t    include!(\"matcher_arms/e.rs\");   // C 2919-3775\n22\t    include!(\"matcher_arms/e2.rs\");  // C 3776-4368\n23\t    include!(\"matcher_arms/e3.rs\");  // C 4369-5248\n24\t    include!(\"matcher_arms/f.rs\");   // C 5249-5488\n25\t    include!(\"matcher_arms/g.rs\");   // C 5489-5988\n26\t    include!(\"matcher_arms/h.rs\");   // C 5989-6569\n27\t    include!(\"matcher_arms/i.rs\");   // C 6570-6890\n28\t\n29\t    return PCRE2_ERROR_INTERNAL;     // the switch's `default:`\n30\t}\n31\t```\n32\t\n33\t`include!` requires each chunk file to be **exactly one block expression**, i.e.\n34\tthe whole file must be:\n35\t\n36\t```rust\n37\t{\n38\t/* ... your statements ... */\n39\t}\n40\t```\n41\t\n42\t(a single outer `{` on the first line and a single matching `}` on the last).\n43\tDo NOT put `use` statements, `fn` items, `static` items or `const` items in a\n44\tchunk file — items are not allowed to be defined in these files because they\n45\twould be re-defined... actually they ARE allowed inside the block (block-scoped\n46\titems), so `static` lookup tables needed by only your chunk may be declared\n47\tinside your block. Prefer that over touching other files.\n48\t\n49\t## The mandatory shape of a chunk file\n50\t\n51\t```rust\n52\t{\n53\tif lbl == LBL_SWITCH {\n54\t    match (*F).op as u32 {\n55\t        OP_CLOSE => {\n56\t            /* ... translated body of `case OP_CLOSE:` ... */\n57\t            lbl = LBL_TOP_OF_LOOP; continue 'sw;   /* == C `break` */\n58\t        }\n59\t        OP_ANY | OP_ALLANY => {\n60\t            ...\n61\t        }\n62\t        _ => {}     /* not one of my opcodes: fall through to the next chunk */\n63\t    }\n64\t}\n65\t\n66\t/* Intra-switch labels that other chunks jump to, if any belong to me: */\n67\tif lbl == LBL_REPEATCHAR {\n68\t    ...\n69\t}\n70\t\n71\t/* RMATCH continuation points that belong to me: */\n72\tif lbl == LBL_RM_BASE + RM1 as u32 {\n73\t    ...\n74\t}\n75\t}\n76\t```\n77\t\n78\t**Every path out of a handled opcode/label must `continue 'sw`, `return`, or\n79\t`break` out of a construct you created yourself. Never fall out of the bottom of\n80\tan `if lbl == ...` block after handling something**, because that would fall into\n81\tthe next chunk.\n82\t\n83\t## Statement translation table\n84\t\n85\t| C | Rust |\n86\t|---|---|\n87\t| `break;` (ending a `case` of the opcode switch) | `lbl = LBL_TOP_OF_LOOP; continue 'sw;` |\n88\t| `continue;` (continue the main `for(;;)` loop)  | `lbl = LBL_TOP_OF_LOOP; continue 'sw;` |\n89\t| `RMATCH(ra, rb);`                               | `start_ecode = ra; (*F).return_id = rb; lbl = LBL_MATCH_RECURSE; continue 'sw;` and the code that follows the RMATCH goes into an `if lbl == LBL_RM_BASE + rb as u32 { ... }` block in your chunk |\n90\t| `RRETURN(ra);`                                  | `rrc = ra; lbl = LBL_RETURN_SWITCH; continue 'sw;` |\n91\t| `goto XXX;` where `XXX:` is in **another** chunk | `lbl = LBL_XXX; continue 'sw;` |\n92\t| `goto XXX;` where `XXX:` is inside **your own** chunk | use a Rust labelled block: wrap the region from the `goto` up to the label in `'xxx: { ... break 'xxx; ... }` so that the code *after* the label still runs in the right enclosing scope. (Do NOT use the `lbl` mechanism for labels that are nested inside blocks, because that loses the enclosing block context.) |\n93\t| `return e;`                                     | `return e;` |\n94\t| `PCRE2_ASSERT(x)` / `PCRE2_DEBUG_UNREACHABLE()` / `PCRE2_UNREACHABLE()` / `PCRE2_FALLTHROUGH` | nothing |\n95\t\n96\tCareful: a `break` inside a `for`/`while`/`do..while`/inner `switch` **inside** a\n97\tcase body breaks that inner construct, not the opcode switch — translate those as\n98\tRust `break` of the corresponding Rust loop, not as `lbl = LBL_TOP_OF_LOOP`.\n99\t\n100\tCareful: a C `continue` inside a `for`/`while` loop inside a case body continues\n101\tthat loop, not the main loop.\n102\t\n103\t## Cross-chunk labels (use the `lbl` mechanism for these only)\n104\t\n105\t| C label | line | constant | lives in chunk |\n106\t|---|---|---|---|\n107\t| `REPEATCHAR`    | 1392 | `LBL_REPEATCHAR` | a |\n108\t| `REPEATNOTCHAR` | 1733 | `LBL_REPEATNOTCHAR` | b |\n109\t| `REPEATTYPE`    | 2973 | `LBL_REPEATTYPE` | e |\n110\t| (split point, C 3776) | 3776 | `LBL_REPEATTYPE_2` | e2 |\n111\t| (split point, C 4369) | 4369 | `LBL_REPEATTYPE_3` | e3 |\n112\t| `REF_REPEAT`    | 5278 | `LBL_REF_REPEAT` | f |\n113\t| `POSSESSIVE_NON_CAPTURE` | 5545 | `LBL_POSSESSIVE_NON_CAPTURE` | g |\n114\t| `POSSESSIVE_CAPTURE`     | 5553 | `LBL_POSSESSIVE_CAPTURE` | g |\n115\t| `POSSESSIVE_GROUP`       | 5557 | `LBL_POSSESSIVE_GROUP` | g |\n116\t| `GROUPLOOP`              | 5676 | `LBL_GROUPLOOP` | g |\n117\t| `ASSERT_NOT_FAILED`      | 5853 | `LBL_ASSERT_NOT_FAILED` | g |\n118\t| `SCS_OFFSET_FOUND`       | 5907 | `LBL_SCS_OFFSET_FOUND` | g |\n119\t| `ASSERT_NL_OR_EOS`       | 6604 | `LBL_ASSERT_NL_OR_EOS` | i |\n120\t\n121\tAll the `goto`s for `REPEATCHAR`, `REPEATNOTCHAR`, `REF_REPEAT`,\n122\t`POSSESSIVE_*`, `GROUPLOOP`, `ASSERT_NOT_FAILED`, `SCS_OFFSET_FOUND` and\n123\t`ASSERT_NL_OR_EOS` happen inside the same chunk as their label, so you may\n124\tinstead use a plain Rust labelled block if that is cleaner — EXCEPT that other\n125\tchunks must still be able to reach a label if a `goto` for it exists outside your\n126\trange. The only genuinely cross-chunk jumps are:\n127\t* `goto REPEATTYPE` at C 2922/2930/2937/2944/2951/2958 (chunk e) → label in\n128\t  chunk e (same chunk, so a labelled block also works)\n129\t* the two artificial split points `LBL_REPEATTYPE_2` (chunk e → e2) and\n130\t  `LBL_REPEATTYPE_3` (chunk e2 → e3).\n131\t\n132\t## Field-name mapping (the `Fxxx` macros)\n133\t\n134\t| C macro | Rust |\n135\t|---|---|\n136\t| `Fback_frame` | `(*F).back_frame` |\n137\t| `Fcapture_last` | `(*F).capture_last` |\n138\t| `Fcurrent_recurse` | `(*F).current_recurse` |\n139\t| `Fecode` | `(*F).ecode` |\n140\t| `Feptr` | `(*F).eptr` |\n141\t| `Fgroup_frame_type` | `(*F).group_frame_type` |\n142\t| `Flast_group_offset` | `(*F).last_group_offset` |\n143\t| `Fmark` | `(*F).mark` |\n144\t| `Frdepth` | `(*F).rdepth` |\n145\t| `Fstart_match` | `(*F).start_match` |\n146\t| `Foffset_top` | `(*F).offset_top` |\n147\t| `Fop` | `(*F).op` (a `u8`; compare with `as u32`) |\n148\t| `Fovector[k]` | `*ovec(F).add(k)`  (`ovec()` is in `matcher_core.rs`) |\n149\t| `Freturn_id` | `(*F).return_id` (a `u8`) |\n150\t| `N->ovector[k]` | `*ovec(N).add(k)` |\n151\t| `P->ovector[k]` | `*ovec(P).add(k)` |\n152\t\n153\tThe `Lxxx` macros that are `#define`d at the point of use inside a case body map\n154\tonto the `fields` union of `heapframe`. E.g.\n155\t\n156\t```\n157\t#define Lstart_eptr  F->fields.char_repeat.start_eptr   ->  (*F).fields.char_repeat.start_eptr\n158\t#define Lcharptr     F->fields.char_repeat.charptr      ->  (*F).fields.char_repeat.charptr\n159\t#define Lmin         F->fields.char_repeat.min          ->  (*F).fields.char_repeat.min\n160\t#define Lmax         F->fields.char_repeat.max          ->  (*F).fields.char_repeat.max\n161\t#define Lc           F->fields.char_repeat.c            ->  (*F).fields.char_repeat.c\n162\t#define Loc          F->fields.char_repeat.oc.oc        ->  (*F).fields.char_repeat.oc.oc\n163\t#define Locchars     F->fields.char_repeat.oc.occu      ->  (*F).fields.char_repeat.oc.occu\n164\t```\n165\tLook at the actual `#define`s in the C right above each case body and translate\n166\tthem to the corresponding `(*F).fields.<variant>.<member>` path. The\n167\t`heapframe`/`hf_fields` definitions are in `src/internal.rs`; the union variant\n168\tstruct names are `hf_char_repeat`, `hf_charnot_repeat`, `hf_class_repeat`,\n169\t`hf_xclass_repeat`, `hf_eclass_repeat`, `hf_type_repeat`, `hf_ref_repeat`,\n170\t`hf_op_bra`, `hf_op_brapos`, `hf_op_recurse`, `hf_op_assert_scs`, `hf_op_cond`,\n171\t`hf_op_vreverse` and the union members are named `char_repeat`,\n172\t`charnot_repeat`, `class_repeat`, `xclass_repeat`, `eclass_repeat`,\n173\t`type_repeat`, `ref_repeat`, `op_bra`, `op_brapos`, `op_recurse`,\n174\t`op_assert_scs`, `op_cond`, `op_vreverse` (all `unsafe` union field accesses).\n175\t`occu` is `[u8; 4]`; index it with `(*F).fields.char_repeat.oc.occu[k]`.\n176\t\n177\t## In-scope local variables (declared in `match_()`)\n178\t\n179\t`F`, `N`, `P` (`*mut heapframe`), `frames_top`, `assert_accept_frame`,\n180\t`frame_copy_size`, `frame_size`, `match_data`, `mb`, `top_bracket`,\n181\t`start_ecode`, `branch_end`, `branch_start`, `bracode` (`PCRE2_SPTR`),\n182\t`offset`, `length` (`PCRE2_SIZE`), `rrc`, `proptype` (`c_int`),\n183\t`i`, `fc`, `number`, `reptype`, `group_frame_type` (`u32`),\n184\t`condition`, `cur_is_word`, `prev_is_word`, `utf`, `ucp` (`BOOL` == `c_int`),\n185\t`lbl` (`u32`).\n186\t\n187\t`mb->xxx` -> `(*mb).